\section{What the passphrase does}{

A worker enrols by presenting a passphrase and receives an opaque session
token in exchange. The passphrase is compared in constant time, and tokens
live only for the life of the host process. This is the only thing that
decides who may contribute work.

Passphrases are deliberately speakable: four words from the EFF long list,
which can be read down a phone line to a colleague who then joins. A
credential that has to be transferred by some other secure channel before it
can be used is a credential most people will work around.
}

\section{What TLS does}{

\code{\link[=host_credentials]{host_credentials()}} generates a certificate and key. Passing the resulting
config to \code{\link[=jobr_serve]{jobr_serve()}} encrypts the channel, and giving workers the client
config lets them confirm they are talking to the host they expect rather than
something that has taken its address.
}

\section{What is not authenticated}{

\strong{Workers are not authenticated by certificate.} Mutual TLS is not available
through nanonext's API: \code{tls_config()} produces either a client config or a
server config, never both, so a listener can be told to require a peer
certificate but cannot be given a certificate authority to validate that
certificate against. Requiring without being able to validate rejects every
client. The project README records the measured matrix behind this.

The passphrase therefore carries the whole weight of deciding who may join.
}

\section{What this means in practice}{

Anyone holding the passphrase can contribute work and can read the jobs they
are handed. Anyone who can read the jobs can see whatever your job parameters
contain.

That is an appropriate model for colleagues, a research group, or friends
lending you their laptops. It is \strong{not} a model for accepting compute from
strangers, and it is not a substitute for device-level identity. If the
machines involved are not ones you would trust with the data in the first
place, put jobR behind something that does authentication properly, or do not
send it that data.

Rotating a passphrase means restarting the host, which invalidates every
outstanding token.
}

\section{What travels}{

Only the files a project's \code{jobR.dcf} manifest lists are bundled and sent, so
nothing in the project directory reaches a worker unless it was named. Job
parameters travel with each chunk, and results travel back. See \link{jobR.dcf}.
}

